# Line endings.
#
# The Windows runner image sets `core.autocrlf=true`, so without this every
# checked-out `*.sh` arrives with CRLF and dies on its shebang — `bash` reads
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` and looks for an interpreter whose name ends in a
# carriage return. That is what the P4 accumulation leg's Windows job works
# around by setting `core.autocrlf=input` before checkout; this makes the
# workaround unnecessary and protects the other 14 scripts in the tree, none of
# which had any reason to expect a Windows checkout until now.
#
# `text=auto` lets git normalise to LF in the index for anything it detects as
# text, and the explicit rules below remove the guesswork for the file types
# where a wrong guess is a broken build rather than a cosmetic diff.
* text=auto

*.sh text eol=lf
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Fixtures compared byte-for-byte. `golden.json` is the cross-platform
# determinism corpus and `demo.jsonl` is the dashboard's committed sample; a
# line-ending rewrite on either is a silent test failure on one platform only.
crates/orrery_conformance/corpus/golden.json -text -eol
gates/p2-dashboard/testdata/*.jsonl -text -eol
